## Insight

For a subarray `nums[i..j]` to be a peak subarray, there must be some peak index `k` with `i < k < j`. Define `peak[k] = 1` if `nums[k] > nums[k-1]` and `nums[k] > nums[k+1]` (for `0 < k < n-1`).

The number of peak subarrays fully inside `[l, r]` equals the number of subarrays `[i, j]` with `l <= i < j <= r`, `j - i >= 2`, that contain at least one peak index strictly inside. Using complement counting:

```
answer = total_subarrays - sum over i of (distance from i to first peak > i)
```

