- Number of peak-containing subarrays in `[l, r]` = sum over each peak `p` of the number of subarrays `[i, j]` where `p` is the **first** peak inside. If `prev[p]` is the previous peak before `p`, then `i` ranges over `(prev[p], p)` intersected with `[l, ...]`, and `j` ranges over `[p+1, r]`. Count = `(min(p, r) - max(prev[p], l-1)) ... ` — this gets complex.

